for text in ['Dear Editors,','Please consider our Article, “'+orig[0]+'”, for npj Genomic Medicine.','Regulatory gene prioritization can change when colocalization inputs and signal models differ. Using primary biliary cholangitis as a fixed disease context, we assembled a traceable PBC-wide screen and evaluated a protocol-frozen high-information subset. The central contribution is an empirical reliability assessment: exact input matching separates support-set and disease-statistic changes from residual bidirectional reclassification, while signal-pair coexistence and covariate sensitivity limit gene-level interpretation.','Truth-known simulations reveal both reduced false sharing and reduced shared-signal recovery, with credible-set discovery reported separately. External disease-study and linked chromatin evidence strengthen the IL12RB2 association within explicit independence and mediation limits. Donor-level liver expression establishes detectability without corrected disease enrichment. This combination is intended to inform defensible functional prioritization; it does not propose a universally superior new algorithm, a newly discovered PBC gene or an established treatment mechanism.','The manuscript is original and is not under consideration elsewhere. The author-confirmed declarations are no specific funding, no competing interests and no additional ethics approval for this secondary analysis of public de-identified data. Generative-AI assistance is disclosed in Methods. Code, aggregate evidence and source provenance are available in the public repository; third-party individual-level inputs are not redistributed.','Corresponding author: Teng Qi\ntengqi@link.cuhk.edu.cn\nSchool of Medicine, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen','Sincerely,\nZhi Chen and Teng Qi']:
 cov.add_paragraph(text)
